  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 AM EDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nEarly this morning, water vapor reveals midlevel troughing over the \nnorth-central CONUS, extending from a midlevel low over the Canadian \nPrairie. At the surface, low pressure is tracking through far \nwestern Ontario, dragging a cold front through the Great Lakes. \nJudging by the surface wind shift, this is already reaching western \nLake Superior. A line of showers and storms has developed ahead of \nit, within the right-rear quadrant of the ~40-50kt LLJ, and showers \nand storms should continue to move through the western UP into the \npre-dawn hours. Most of the western UP should see totals of a tenth \nof an inch or less, but will not rule out some higher amounts closer \nto 0.20in where thunderstorms can hold together. CAMs continue to \nshow showers falling apart with eastward extent as they contend with \na drier airmass. However, convection may re-fire over the far \neastern UP as we destabilize ahead of the front, but strong storms \nare not expected with lackluster CAPE and shear.  \n\nBehind the cold front, modest pressure rises and cold air advection \nwill make for an otherwise breezy and cooler day. Expect an \notherwise cooler day, as temperatures starting in the upper 50s and \nlower 60s climb to highs in the lower to mid 70s for most. Breezy \nwesterlies will be the rule with gusts 25-35 mph common in the \nKeweenaw, and up to 20-25mph elsewhere. Sunday, we can't rule out \nsome scattered lake effect rain showers or at least lake effect \ncloud cover for the NW wind snowbelt as 850 mb temps fall below 5C \n(0-2C per NAM or GFS) while the lake average surface temp sits at \n~17C. Some of the CAMs are showing somewhat more widespread lake \neffect rain coverage, and even hints for a banded structure pointing \ninto Munising that may be able to provide upwards of a quarter inch \nof rain. That said, with a lot of dry air noted in area soundings, \nand soundings only showing decent mixing to around 6-8kft, these \nhigher-end amounts seem rather ambitious. \n\nLooking into early next week, ensembles continue to hone in on a \ndeepening trough over the Rockies ejecting a deepening surface low \nfrom the northern Plains northeast through Ontario Monday PM through \nTuesday. Operational deterministic models and their ensemble \ncounterparts are coming into better agreement regarding the track \nand timing of the surface low pressure feature, but there still \nremains disagreement between each ensemble and their individual \nensemble members. As the trough lifts north of Lake Superior, strong \nisentropic ascent within the warm sector will be the focal point for \nshower and thunderstorms development. With considerable northward \nmoisture transport (PWATS near 1.75-2\", the 95-99th percentile of \nclimatology), widespread moderate to heavy rain is possible. Across \nthe southern half of the UP, the NBM suggests a 50-75% chance for \nQPF >1.0\" by Tuesday morning, however, this may be high biased as \nindividual ensembles and the LREF barely scrapes 15-30%. \nAdditionally, gusty winds are expected both Monday and Tuesday.",
